Package: mos-wifi-ap
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: hostapd, rfkill, wireless-regdb, iw
Description: mos Wi-Fi access-point support
 Masks hostapd.service by symlinking it to /dev/null, and ships the
 etc-hostapd.mount that binds /etc/hostapd from the STATE partition together
 with its local-fs.target.wants link.
 .
 hostapd@.service is left INSTALLED AND UNENABLED. mosd's access-point
 reconciler starts the instance for the interface it brought up and renders
 that instance's configuration file; the distribution's non-templated unit
 would start a second hostapd against a configuration nothing wrote.
 .
 The bind mount is not a convenience. On the mos image /etc is inside the
 read-only dm-verity squashfs, so without it the provisioning access point --
 the path to a device's first configuration -- never comes up, and the AP
 pre-shared key would not survive an A/B update.
 .
 Station support is mos-wifi, a separate package. A board that only serves an
 access point installs this one alone, and a board that only joins networks
 installs neither hostapd nor this package. rfkill is required because the
 radio is soft-blocked out of the box on the boards this image targets.
 .
 wireless-regdb and iw are required for the reason mos-wifi's description
 gives, and they matter more here: hostapd advertises the regulatory domain
 (ieee80211d=1) and takes its channel and transmit-power limits from it, so an
 unloaded database is an AP beaconing under cfg80211's built-in world domain
 whatever country_code was configured. The unit that performs the load,
 mos-regdb-reload.service, is mos-wifi's payload -- a path has one owner -- and
 rootfs/packages/radio-wifi.pkgs names both packages for every board that
 declares the wifi radio, so no resolution this tree produces has this package
 without it.
 .
 Architecture: all -- the payload is one unit file and two symlinks, and it
 carries no ELF whose shared-library dependencies a packer could resolve.
